Now I need to understand the actual bug. Let me look at the `hb_hashmap_t` constructor and initialization more carefully. The `packed_map` is a member of `hb_serialize_context_t`. When the serializer is constructed, `packed_map` is default-initialized. But the issue is that `hb_hashmap_t`'s constructor calls `init()` which calls `hb_object_init(this)` and `init_shallow()`.

Wait, let me look at the `hb_hashmap_t` init again:

```cpp
void init_shallow ()
{
    successful = true;
    population = occupancy = 0;
    mask = 0;
    prime = 0;
    items = nullptr;
}
void init ()
{
    hb_object_init (this);
    init_shallow ();
}
```

And `hb_object_init` sets `ref_count`, `writable`, and `user_data`. But `hb_hashmap_t` is not a heap-allocated object with `hb_object_header_t header` - wait, yes it is:

```cpp
hb_object_header_t header;
```

So `hb_object_init` initializes the header. But the issue is that `hb_hashmap_t` is used as a member of `hb_serialize_context_t`, and when the serializer is reset, `packed_map.fini()` is called, then later `packed_map` is used again. Let me check the `fini` method:

```cpp
void fini ()
{
    hb_object_fini (this);
    fini_shallow ();
}
```

And `fini_shallow`:
```cpp
void fini_shallow ()
{
    free (items);
    items = nullptr;
    population = occupancy = 0;
}
```

So after `fini()`, the items are freed. But then when `packed_map` is used again (after `reset()` which calls `fini()`), the items pointer is nullptr. The `get` method checks `if (unlikely (!items)) return vINVALID;` so that should be fine.

But wait - the `reset()` method of `hb_serialize_context_t` calls `fini()` on `packed_map`, but then doesn't re-initialize it! Let me check:

```cpp
void reset ()
{
    this->successful = true;
    this->ran_out_of_room = false;
    this->head = this->start;
    this->tail = this->end;
    this->debug_depth = 0;

    fini ();
    this->packed.push (nullptr);
}
```

And `fini()`:
```cpp
void fini ()
{
    ++ hb_iter (packed)
    | hb_apply ([] (object_t *_) { _->fini (); })
    ;
    packed.fini ();
    this->packed_map.fini ();

    while (current)
    {
        auto *_ = current;
        current = current->next;
        _->fini ();
    }
    object_pool.fini ();
}
```

So `reset()` calls `fini()` which calls `packed_map.fini()`. After `fini()`, the `packed_map` is in a finalized state (items = nullptr, etc.). But then `reset()` continues to use the serializer, and `pop_pack()` calls `packed_map.get(obj)` which should be fine since items is nullptr.

But wait - the issue is that `reset()` is called from the constructor too:

```cpp
hb_serialize_context_t (void *start_, unsigned int size) :
    start ((char *) start_),
    end (start + size),
    current (nullptr)
{ reset (); }
```

The constructor initializes `start`, `end`, `current` but NOT `packed`, `packed_map`, `object_pool`. Then `reset()` calls `fini()` which tries to call methods on uninitialized members!

Actually, looking more carefully at the constructor, the members `packed`, `packed_map`, `object_pool` are default-constructed before the constructor body runs. So they should be properly initialized.

Let me think about this differently. The MSAN error is "Use-of-uninitialized-value" in `hb_hashmap_t`. Let me look at the `resize()` method:

```cpp
bool resize ()
{
    if (unlikely (!successful)) return false;

    unsigned int power = hb_bit_storage (population * 2 + 8);
    unsigned int new_size = 1u << power;
    item_t *new_items = (item_t *) malloc ((size_t) new_size * sizeof (item_t));
    if (unlikely (!new_items))
    {
        successful = false;
        return false;
    }
    + hb_iter (new_items, new_size)
    | hb_apply (&item_t::clear)
    ;

    unsigned int old_size = mask + 1;
    item_t *old_items = items;

    /* Switch to new, empty, array. */
    population = occupancy = 0;
    mask = new_size - 1;
    prime = prime_for (power);
    items = new_items;

    /* Insert back old items. */
    if (old_items)
        for (unsigned int i = 0; i < old_size; i++)
            if (old_items[i].is_real ())
                set (old_items[i].key, old_items[i].value);

    free (old_items);

    return true;
}
```

The `resize()` function allocates new items, clears them, then re-inserts old items. The `set` method computes the hash of the key. For `object_t*` keys, this calls `object_t::hash()`.

Now, the key insight: when `pop_pack()` is called, it does:
```cpp
objidx_t objidx = packed_map.get (obj);
```

This calls `get` with the `obj` pointer as key. The hashmap computes `hb_hash(key)` which calls `object_t::hash()` on the object. But at this point, the object's `links` vector may have been modified or the object may have been freed and reallocated from the pool.

Wait, let me re-read `pop_pack()`:

```cpp
objidx_t pop_pack ()
{
    object_t *obj = current;
    if (unlikely (!obj)) return 0;
    current = current->next;
    obj->tail = head;
    obj->next = nullptr;
    unsigned len = obj->tail - obj->head;
    head = obj->head; /* Rewind head. */

    if (!len)
    {
        assert (!obj->links.length);
        return 0;
    }

    objidx_t objidx = packed_map.get (obj);
    if (objidx)
    {
        obj->fini ();
        return objidx;
    }

    tail -= len;
    memmove (tail, obj->head, len);

    obj->head = tail;
    obj->tail = tail + len;

    packed.push (obj);

    if (unlikely (packed.in_error ()))
        return 0;

    objidx = packed.length - 1;

    packed_map.set (obj, objidx);

    return objidx;
}
```

So `packed_map.get(obj)` is called with the object pointer. The hashmap computes `hb_hash(key)` which calls `object_t::hash()`. The `hash()` method accesses `links.as_bytes()`. The `links` vector has been used during serialization (links were added via `add_link`), so it should be properly initialized.

But wait - what if the object was popped from the pool and the `links` vector's internal data (allocated via `push()`) contains uninitialized padding bytes? The `hb_vector_t::push()` allocates memory with `realloc`, which doesn't zero-initialize the memory. So the `as_bytes()` call would include uninitialized padding bytes.

